 3. Exclusive: Trump adviser on ratepayer shields 
 
 By Avery Lotz and Ben Geman 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The Trump administration's Jarrod Agen in an interview with Axios' Amy Harder on Sept. 22. Screenshot: Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A push to protect Americans from footing the bill for the AI buildout is one of the "bottom lines" in negotiations on permitting for energy projects, the White House's Jarrod Agen said at an Axios House event yesterday.

 The big picture: Senate negotiators are nearing a bipartisan permitting deal, Sen. Brian Schatz (D-Hawai'i) told Axios, bringing lawmakers closer to a key legislative priority for business groups looking to cut red tape. 

 But Democrats' demand for assurances that the administration won't impede renewable projects could strain those talks. What he's saying: "There needs to be protection so that the AI development is not passed on to the ratepayer," Agen, Trump's National Energy Dominance Council director, told Axios' Amy Harder.

 "You cannot look at our grid and infrastructure without factoring that," he added. The data centers powering the AI boom — with massive amounts of energy — have emerged as a defining political issue with stark public opposition. Friction point: A permitting deal isn't guaranteed.

 Schatz cautioned to Axios' Ben Geman on Monday that lawmakers need to understand whether the administration is "going to lift its ban on solar and wind energy," citing the Pentagon's refusal to provide once-routine sign-offs on wind permits. 
 
